# Commit messages

When asked to propose or write a commit message (or after summarizing repo
changes for a commit), follow this.

## Inspect changes

1. Run `git status` and `git diff --cached` (or `git diff` if nothing is
   staged) to see what would be committed.
2. Summarize the changes accurately; do not invent scope or files.

## Format (Conventional Commits)

Output **one** plaintext fenced code block containing the **full** message
only. Do not wrap the subject or body in extra commentary outside that block.

- **Subject:** one line; prefer ~**50** characters; never more than **72**
- Blank line after the subject
- **Body and bullets:** hard-wrap at **72** characters; indent wrapped list
  continuations

```
type(scope): brief description

- Change 1
- Change 2 that runs long and continues on the next line with
  matching indentation when needed
```

Use a real `type` (`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`, `style`) and
an optional scope (`home`, `support`, `privacy`, `css`, `history`, `cursor`,
`resources`). Imperative subject; bullets for substance.

## Repo policy

Follow `.cursor/rules/git-workflow.mdc` for when to commit.

- **Handoff / implementation:** when implementing from `docs/handoffs/` or
  when the user asks to build and keep git updated, `git add` narrowly and
  `git commit` after each logical milestone.
- **Otherwise:** do not run `git commit`, `git add`, or `git stash` unless
  the user explicitly asked. Inspecting with `git status` / `git diff` is
  always fine.
